PROLOGUE

Gray clouds sagged low over Helvanthor, swollen and heavy, pressing down upon the slate rooftops of the capital. What little light slipped through them came thin and colorless, washing the lower market in shades of ash and mud. Carts with warped wheels lined the narrow street, their awnings patched with sackcloth and canvas faded to the color of dishwater. Merchants called out prices to passersby who could not pay them. Smoke from cookfires drifted between the stalls, mingling with the sour tang of spoiled cabbage and the stink of the gutters running along both sides of the cobbles.

Dusty leaned against the corner post of a bread stall, the hood of his cloak drawn low over his brow. The cloak had been brown once. Now it was the color of the street, frayed at the hem and stiff with old grime. The rags beneath hung loose from his wiry frame, cinched at the waist with a knotted length of twine. Even slouched, he stood a head taller than most who shuffled past, and so he folded himself down, shoulders rounded, weight settled into the post until he was little more than another shadow beneath the awning.

"You're glaring at them again." Old Bram didn't look up from the loaves he was stacking, his knuckles swollen and spotted, flour caked into the creases of his palms. "Glare any harder and you'll scare off the ones with coin."

"I'm sorry." Dusty's black eyes never left the crowd. "I'll try to glare less at the ones with coin."

Bram snorted, a wheezing sound that broke apart into a cough. He wiped his mouth on his sleeve, then wrinkled his nose as the wind shifted and carried Dusty's smell across the stall. "Gods above, boy. When's the last time you stood in the rain?"

"Tuesday, sir."

"Stand in another."

Dusty said nothing. His gaze moved over the market in slow sweeps, the way it always did. A boy loitered near the rain barrel with his hands tucked under his arms. A girl in a torn shift stood beside the fishmonger's cart, watching the fishmonger and not the fish. Two city watchmen in dented half-helms leaned against the far wall by the well, one of them picking at his teeth, neither of them watching anything at all. That was all Helvanthor spared for its lower market. Two men and a wall to lean against.

Then Dusty saw her.

She came up the street from the east end, and the crowd bent around her the way water bends around a stone. An elf. He had seen perhaps three in his life, and always at a distance, always riding past in carriages with curtains drawn. This one walked. Her hair was a pale, soft pink, gathered into a thick braid that fell to her waist, though a dozen wisps had worked themselves loose to float about her face in the damp air. A green cloak hung from her shoulders, fine wool gone thin at the elbows and muddied along the hem. Beneath it she wore a thick woolen blouse buttoned up to her neck, the fabric drawn tight across her chest and hugging the narrow curve of her waist, and baggy traveling pants tucked into heeled brown boots scuffed pale at the toes.

Two men followed a pace behind her, and they were not city watch.

They wore torso plate that gleamed dully even under the gray sky, helmets that covered all but their eyes and mouths, and boots of solid metal that rang against the cobbles with every step. Heavy spears rested against their shoulders. Large shields hung across their backs, each one painted with a white tower against a field of deep blue. The people of the lower market took one look at them and found somewhere else to be.

"Academy men," Bram muttered, following his gaze. "Mythrandis. What in the gods' names are they doing down here?"

Dusty didn't know, so he said nothing.

The elf stopped at the potter's stall and picked up a cracked jug, turned it over, set it down. She moved to the cobbler and stared at his hands while he worked, close enough that the cobbler leaned away from her. She moved on to the fishmonger, to the tinker, to the woman selling bruised apples from a basket. She bought nothing. She only looked, her light blue eyes wide and searching, her lips moving without pause. Dusty caught fragments of it when the wind was right.

"No, no, that's just — too dull, and that one's — no. Honestly, there has to be at least —"

The boy by the rain barrel chose that moment to make his run.

He was quick, small and barefoot, darting low along the edge of the stall with his hand already reaching for the nearest loaf. Dusty didn't move from the post. He only raised his left hand from beneath the folds of his cloak and lifted one finger, and a flame bloomed at its tip, no larger than a thumbnail. Around his fingers the air darkened, a faint haze of black edged in a thread of silver, there and gone again as the flame steadied.

The boy froze with his hand outstretched. His eyes went to the flame, then climbed up to the shadowed face beneath the hood.

"Please don't," Dusty said.

The boy ran. Dusty let the flame burn a moment longer before closing his fist around it.

Bram grunted and nudged the loaf back into line. "That's one."

The second was older, a teenager with a split lip and a crooked nose, and he was cleverer about it. He came from the other side with a friend, and the friend knocked over a basket of onions at the neighboring stall and started shouting. Half the street turned to look. Dusty didn't. By the time the teen's hand closed around a loaf, Dusty had already stepped off the post and put himself between the teen and the street, and the flame was burning two inches from the teen's eye.

The heat touched his lashes. The teen went very still.

"Put it back, please."

The teen set the loaf down. He spat at Dusty's feet, then turned and shoved his way into the crowd, his friend abandoning the onions to follow him.

"Two," Bram said.

Dusty returned to his post.

The third came near midmorning, when the clouds had darkened and the first damp mist began to settle over the market. He was a man, or had been once. Sores wept along his jaw and the side of his neck, yellow at the edges, and his eyes were rheumy and red-rimmed beneath a brow slick with fever sweat. He coughed as he shuffled forward, a wet, rattling sound that made the woman beside him clutch her shawl over her mouth and hurry away. He didn't try to be quick. He didn't try to be clever. He simply walked up to the stall and reached out a trembling, blistered hand.

Bram flinched back from the counter.

Dusty's flame was already lit. "Sir. Please stop."

The man's hand kept moving. His cracked lips parted, and a thin sound came out, not a word, only need.

"Sir."

The man's fingers closed around the loaf.

Dusty pressed the flame to the back of his hand.

The skin hissed. A sharp, sweet smell rose from it, and the man howled, jerking his arm back and dropping the loaf onto the cobbles. He clutched his burned hand to his chest and stumbled backward, his heel catching the gutter, and he went down hard into the filth. Nobody moved to help him. After a moment he dragged himself up and limped away, still howling, the sound thinning out as the crowd swallowed him.

Dusty closed his fist. The flame died.

When he looked up, the elf was staring at him.

She stood across the street beside the apple seller, her head tilted to one side, the loose wisps of her pink hair drifting across her face. Her eyes weren't on his face. They were on his hand, on the fist he'd closed, and they were wider than before, if that was possible. Her lips had stopped moving. Behind her, one of the Mythrandis guards leaned down and said something to her, and she didn't answer him.

Dusty turned his back to her and returned to his post.

Bram crouched with a groan and picked up the loaf the sick man had dropped. He looked at it for a long moment. Then he tossed it into the gutter.

When Dusty glanced back across the street, the elf was gone.

---

The noon bell rang out from the cathedral on the hill, its tolling muffled by the clouds. Bram began pulling his awning down, and Dusty helped him lash the canvas without being asked. When they had finished, the old man reached beneath the counter and pulled out two loaves, day-old, the crusts gone hard and pale.

"Same as always." He pressed them into Dusty's hands. "Same time tomorrow, if you're not dead."

"Thank you, sir." Dusty tucked the bread beneath his cloak and pressed it tight against his ribs with his forearm. "I'll try not to be."

He left the lower market by the south road, where the cobbles gave way to packed dirt, and the dirt gave way to mud.

---

They called it the Greys.

It had a proper name once, on a map somewhere in a city office that none of its people would ever see. Now it was only the Greys, for the color of the walls, the color of the sky above them, and the color of the people who lived between them. Buildings leaned against one another like drunks, their upper floors sagging over the lanes until the gray sky narrowed into a thin, crooked stripe overhead. The cold here was a damp cold, the kind that soaked through cloth and settled into bone. Water dripped from broken gutters and pooled in the ruts of the lane, black and oily and still. Refuse gathered in every corner, rotted cabbage leaves and fish bones and rags too filthy even for the Greys, and over all of it hung a stench so thick it coated the back of the throat.

Bodies huddled in the doorways and beneath the overhangs. Old women wrapped in sacking. Men with hollow cheeks and empty hands. Children pressed together for warmth in piles like puppies. They didn't speak as Dusty passed. They only watched, and he felt their eyes drop to the bulge beneath his cloak, felt the want in them, sharp and patient.

He walked down the center of the lane, away from the doorways, away from the mouths of the alleys. His steps were careful, measured, his weight balanced on the balls of his feet. His arm stayed tight across his ribs. His other hand hung loose at his side, fingers relaxed, ready.

He made it three streets before they came.

They emerged from the shadows of a collapsed tenement, slipping out from beneath fallen beams and through gaps in crumbling brick. Ten of them, or near enough. None older than eleven or twelve. Their faces were smeared with soot and their clothes hung in tatters, and in their small fists they gripped whatever the Greys had given them. A length of rotted wood, soft and black at one end. Rocks pried loose from the lane. Clods of hard-packed dirt and dried mud.

The one in front was a girl with a shaved head and a scar through her eyebrow. She looked at the bulge under his cloak. Then she looked at him.

"Give it," she said.

"I'm sorry," Dusty said. "I can't."

She threw her rock.

Dusty twisted and it glanced off his shoulder instead of his skull. A clod of dirt burst against his hood, spraying grit into his eyes, and then the rest of them were on him, all at once, screaming. He lit his flame. He didn't have time to make it more than it was, a small bright tongue of orange at the tip of one finger with that black, silver-edged haze about his knuckles, but he didn't need it to be more. He swept it across the face of the first boy to reach him and the boy shrieked and fell back clawing at his eyes. A girl came at him with her mouth wide open, screaming, and Dusty jabbed the flame straight into it. She choked and dropped to her knees, retching, her hands at her lips.

The length of rotted wood cracked across his back. It broke on impact, soft wood splintering, and Dusty spun into the boy who had swung it and drove his elbow into his nose. Blood sprayed. The boy went down.

A rock struck his knee. Another hit his ribs, just beneath the bread, and he grunted and curled his arm tighter. Small hands grabbed at his cloak, at his legs, at the twine around his waist. He stamped down on a bare foot and heard a yelp. He swept his leg out and took two of them off their feet at once, sending them sprawling into a black puddle. He thrust the flame at a face, then another, then another, aiming for eyes, for open mouths, anywhere that made them flinch and hesitate. He never held it long. He didn't need to. The fear did the rest.

This was not his first fight in the Greys. It was not his twentieth.

The girl with the shaved head came at him again, a jagged rock in each fist. Dusty let her come. He stepped inside her swing, caught her wrist, and turned it until the rock fell from her fingers. Then he held his flame beneath her chin, close enough that the heat reddened her skin.

She stared up at him. He stared back.

"Please," he said. "Go home."

He let her go.

She backed away from him, one step, then two, her eyes never leaving the flame. The others were already pulling back, the ones who could still stand dragging the ones who couldn't. A boy sobbed with his hands pressed over his eyes. The girl he'd burned was still spitting blood into the mud. They gathered themselves at the mouth of the collapsed tenement, and for a moment they only looked at him, ten small, dirty faces full of hate and hunger.

Then the girl with the shaved head turned and ran, and the rest of them ran with her, scattering back into the shadows they had come from.

Dusty closed his fist. The flame went out.

He stood in the middle of the lane with his chest heaving, his breath coming in ragged gasps that fogged in the cold air. His knee throbbed where the rock had struck it. His back ached from the wood. Grit still burned in the corners of his eyes. His mana hadn't dimmed, not even a flicker; it never did. His lungs were another matter.

He shifted his arm and felt the two loaves beneath his cloak. Still there. Still whole.

He started walking.

"Halt."

The voice came from behind him, deep and hard and used to being obeyed. Metal rang against stone. Dusty turned slowly.

A Mythrandis guard stepped out from the mouth of an alley, his spear lowered, its heavy point leveled at Dusty's chest. The white tower on his shield was spattered with mud from the lanes. Behind him came the second guard, spear still on his shoulder, his lip curled beneath the rim of his helmet as he took in the Greys around him.

Every muscle in Dusty's body went tight. Guards didn't come to the Greys. Not unless they came for something. His weight shifted to the balls of his feet and his hand drifted to his side, and his eyes flicked past the guard to the lanes behind him, measuring the distance, counting the turns.

"Oh — no, no, don't point that at him, Garrick, honestly, he's not — put it down. Up. Put it up."

The voice was softer, lighter, and it came from behind the guard's shoulder. Then the elf stepped around him, her green cloak swirling about her ankles, her boots splashing carelessly through a black puddle. She laid her hand on the shaft of the guard's spear and pushed it aside as though it were a branch across a forest path. The guard let her.

"Could you — the people, there," she gestured vaguely at the doorways, at the huddled bodies watching from beneath the overhangs, "and there, and those ones, would you just — move them? Back? A little? I don't want — it's hard to think with everyone — please."

The guard hesitated. Then he turned and began barking at the doorways, and the bodies there stirred and shuffled deeper into the shadows.

The elf turned back to Dusty and stepped closer.

He stepped back.

She didn't seem to notice. She took another step, and he took another, until his heel struck the wall of a building behind him and there was nowhere left to go. She stopped an arm's length from him, and up close she was smaller than he'd thought, the top of her head barely reaching his chin. Her eyes were very blue and very wide, and they moved over him from hood to boots, as though he were a page she meant to memorize.

"I'm Valia. Valia Velanthus." She pushed a loose strand of pink hair from her face and it fell right back. "And you are?"

Dusty said nothing.

"Your name. You have one? Everyone has one, I would think, even — sorry, I mean, not even, just — you have one."

Dusty said nothing.

The second guard's metal boots rang against the cobbles as he stepped around her, his spear coming down off his shoulder. "The lady asked you a question, rat. You'll answer her with respect or you'll answer me without teeth."

Valia flapped a hand at him without looking, the way someone might wave away a fly. "Oh, stop it, Dace, you'll — go stand over there. By the — yes. There."

The guard's jaw tightened beneath his helmet. He stepped back.

"Sorry. About him. About them." Valia glanced back at the two men, then at Dusty again. "They're not — I'm not anyone, really, I'm not nobility or a magistrate or — I'm a researcher. At Mythrandis. The academy, you've heard of the academy? I study mana, how it — well, a lot of things about how it. And they're not mine, they're only there to guard me, you know, keep me from getting stabbed, which apparently I'm very likely to get, according to them." She tilted her head. "The way you were guarding the bread man. This morning. It's the same thing. Really exactly the same thing."

Dusty looked at her. Then he looked at the two guards, at their gleaming plate, their spears, the mud splashed across their painted shields. He thought of Bram's swollen knuckles, of two day-old loaves pressed into his palms.

It was the same thing.

"Dusty," he said.

"Dusty." She tasted it. "Dusty. That's — is that a real — it doesn't matter. It's good. Dusty." She leaned in closer, and he pressed his back against the wall. "Your hand. This morning. The fire. It was so small."

"Yes, ma'am. It's all I have."

"No, no, small is — small's fine, small's nothing, half the city's small, that's not the — it was black." Her hands came up, her fingers curling in the air as though she could shape it there between them. "Around your fingers. Black. And the edge of it was — silver? Was it silver? It was silver. I'm almost sure it was silver. I saw it again just now, from the alley, when you — the children, with the —" She waved at the mud, the scattered rocks, the broken halves of rotted wood.

Dusty looked down at his hand. He had seen the darkness there his whole life. He had never thought of it as anything but his own.

"I didn't know it was unusual, ma'am."

"Unusual? It's — I've never seen it. Not once. Not anywhere. I've read about — no, I haven't, I haven't read about it either, that's the — do you know what that means?"

"No, ma'am."

"No. No, neither do I." She seized his wrist before he could stop her and turned his hand over, peering at his palm, at his long, dirty fingers, as if the answer might be written there in the grime. Her grip was stronger than he expected. "When did it start? The fire, I mean, not the — well, both. Did someone teach you? Did it come on its own? When you pull on it, what does it feel like, does it feel like a well, or a pond, or — can you feel the bottom?"

Dusty pulled his hand free. "I'm sorry. I don't know how to answer that."

"Have you ever run out?"

"No, ma'am."

Her eyes went even wider. "Never? Not once? Not even when — you just fought ten children, you were lighting it over and over and over, you must have — nothing? You didn't feel it drain at all?"

"Not that I remember."

"But that's — that can't — it was so small, it should have been gone in —" She let out a frustrated little sound and seized the end of her braid in her fist, twisting it. "But how can you not know? Hasn't anyone ever — no, of course nobody's ever tested you, why would anyone down here — gods, it's like trying to read a book with the pages glued together." She tugged at the braid. "I can't do anything with this here. Nothing. I don't have my — any of my — I'd need the lab, and a proper resonance stone, and at least three weeks of —"

She stopped. She stared at the wall beside his head. Her lips kept moving, softer now, and he realized she was no longer talking to him.

"If I had him close, though. The east room would work, it's got the — no, the Maids would never let a — but if he were a student, then technically he'd be — the lottery. The lottery! It's the — there's always a spot, isn't there, there's always one or two that — and the Headmaster always says yes, he never says no to me, not once, not ever, so there's no reason he'd —" She gave a small, sharp nod, as though someone had asked her a question and she had answered it. "Yes. Yes, that's — yes."

Her eyes snapped back to Dusty.

"How would you like to attend a school?"

Dusty opened his mouth. Nothing came out of it.

"A magic school. Mythrandis. You'd study, and you'd — well, you'd have a room, and meals, and classes, and I could — I'd be there, I'd help, obviously, and we could figure out what that —" She gestured at his hand again. "What that is."

The Greys dripped around them. Somewhere down the lane, a baby cried. Dusty stood with his back against the cold, wet wall, two loaves of stale bread pressed against his ribs, and he looked down at the elf who had just offered him the world, and he could not think of a single thing to say.

He had imagined many things in his life. He had imagined a warm room. He had imagined a full stomach. He had never once imagined this.

"Thank you, ma'am," he said at last. "I'm sorry. I can't. I have other responsibilities."

"Oh, that's fine." She waved it away. "I'll just talk to your parents. Parents always come around once they hear what the — where are they? Can we go now? Is it close?"

"The guards come through the Greys every few years," Dusty said. "When the coughing sickness spreads. They seal the streets where it's worst, and they clear everyone inside. So it won't reach the rest of the city." He said it plainly, the way he'd say the price of bread. "My parents were on one of those streets. Eight years ago."

Valia went still.

Every part of her, all at once. Her hands stopped moving. Her lips stopped moving. For half a breath she didn't even blink, her wide blue eyes fixed on his face with nothing moving behind them at all.

Then it broke, and horror flooded in. "They — the guards? The city's guards? They just — and the crown allows —" She turned to look back at Garrick and Dace, as if they might explain it to her, then turned back. "That's — no. No. Then you're coming with me. You have to. You can't stay here, not with — you have to come."

"I'm sorry, ma'am. I can't."

"Why?" Her voice climbed, high and sharp and bewildered. She flung her arm out at the lane, at the refuse heaped in the corners, at the black water in the ruts, at the hollow-eyed faces peering from the doorways. "What could possibly — what's so important that you'd stay — here? In this? Instead of — I'm offering you a way out of it, a real one, what could be worth —"

"My sister is sick."

The words came out quiet, and they stopped her.

"She's twelve. She's been sick for a long time. I'm all she has." He met her eyes. "I won't leave her."

Valia's face softened. The sharpness drained from it, and the bewilderment with it, and what was left behind was something gentler that she didn't seem to know was showing. She looked at him for a long moment, at the ragged hood, the mud-splattered cloak, the arm still pressed protectively across the bread he'd fought ten children to keep.

"A sister," she said softly.

"Yes, ma'am."

"Oh." She chewed her full lower lip. Then her eyes brightened, and her hands started moving again. "Oh, but that's — that's easy. That's not even — there's a city. Aethres. At the end of the bridge, on the mainland side, it's right there, you can see the towers from its streets. I could get her a room. Probably. Almost certainly. And you could visit her between your studies, on rest days, it's not far at all, the Guard escorts students across all the time —"

Dusty's eyes narrowed. He turned the offer over, searching for its edges, for the hook buried in the bait. There was always a hook.

Valia saw it and pressed on. "And I know the Headmaster. Well, I don't know him, nobody really knows him, but he — he listens to me. He always says yes. And the academy has healers, the best in the world, truly, I could have a few of them look at her. At your sister. They could find out what's wrong."

Dusty said nothing. His gaze slid past her shoulder to the two guards. Garrick stood with his spear planted in the mud, his nose wrinkled beneath the rim of his helmet. Dace had turned his head to spit, and when he turned back his eyes found Dusty and stayed there, full of open disgust, as though he'd found something foul on the sole of his boot and didn't know how to scrape it off.

Dusty looked at them and he thought of spears leveled at the mouths of sealed streets. He thought of a place full of men like them, and boys who would grow into men like them, and girls who would look at him the way Dace was looking at him now.

Valia followed his gaze and, for once, seemed to understand it.

"You could learn it yourself," she said.

His eyes came back to her.

"Healing." She stepped closer, and this time he didn't step back. "You could learn healing. It's one of the disciplines, one of the three, and you — with that, with a flame that doesn't go out — do you know what a healer would give for that? Healing isn't about how much you have, not really, it's about how long you can hold it steady. How long you can keep pouring. And you never run out." Her voice dropped, and she spoke slowly. "You could take care of her. Yourself. With your own two hands."

Dusty was quiet for a long moment. The baby down the lane had stopped crying. Water dripped from a broken gutter somewhere above them.

He thought of Rinnie's hands shaking under her blanket. He thought of cold nights with her curled against his chest, and his own mana held steady in his palm, a small warmth against her back until the gray light of morning came.

"I'll come," he said.

Valia smiled.

It spread across her whole face, unguarded and bright, and it reached all the way up into her wide blue eyes. She turned and clapped her hands once, the sound echoing flat off the damp walls.

"Garrick! Dace! We'll be — there'll be more of us. On the way back. Two more." She beamed at them. "New travelers."

The two guards exchanged a look beneath their helmets. Neither of them said a word.

Valia turned back to Dusty, and she folded her hands together in front of her, and the wild energy settled around her for a moment into something almost formal. Almost careful.

"Would you take me to her, please? Your sister?"

Dusty looked at her. Then he pushed himself off the wall, shifting the two loaves of bread against his ribs, and pulled his hood a little lower.

"Will her room have a window?" he asked. "One the sun comes through?"
